<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="3522f2f0"></attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="1a25928b"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="b02579ec">&lt;p&gt;
    The main goal of the rollback plan is to maintain business operations.&amp;nbsp; Look closely at what is needed to deliver
    a minimum level of service and functionality, or to revert to the previous level. Keep the plan simple – use clear,
    plain language that will be easily understood by the varied groups of users from the business and technology.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Identify the trigger – what, specifically, will cause the need to implement the plan? Decide which actions need to
        be taken, and when. Determine who is in charge at each stage and what type of reporting process they must
        follow.&amp;nbsp;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Identify everyone&apos;s needs – have people throughout the company identify what they must have, at a minimum, to
        continue operations.&amp;nbsp;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Define time periods – What must be done during the first hour of the plan being implemented? The first day? The
        first week? If the team looks at the plan in this way, they&apos;re less likely to leave out important details.&amp;nbsp;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Review the current state operating model when determining fail-over. What are the critical operations needed to
        maintain function and support?&amp;nbsp;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Consider related resource restrictions – will the organization be able to function the same way if it’s necessary
        to implement a Plan B? Will Plan B necessarily reduce capabilities?&amp;nbsp;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Define &apos;success&apos; – what activities are necessary to return to normal operations?&amp;nbsp;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Manage risks – look for opportunities to reduce risk wherever possible. This may help reduce, or even eliminate,
        the need for full contingency plans in certain areas.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    It is also critical to review lessons learned from an executed roll-back process as it may&amp;nbsp;yield missed or
    under-estimated risks that can be planned for in the current circumstance. Whether a new plan is developed or based on
    or updated with an existing or previous plan,&amp;nbsp;it should be tested prior to the deployment event to determine
    viability and feasibility.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Ideally, an organization should update disaster recovery process and procedures in line with the transition. This may
    not be a requirement of the transition or implementation, but disaster recovery should not be viewed as a solution for
    fail-over even if the previous state disaster recovery environment is still in place.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Include fail-over plans in standard operating procedures – make sure initial training is provided on the plan, and
    keep&amp;nbsp;all stakeholders&amp;nbsp;up-to-date on changes.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    It is important to note that a fail-over process is not a disaster recovery contingency&amp;nbsp;component. Although there
    are some similarities in the functions, disaster recovery is not a fail-over solution.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Companies concerned with meeting service level agreements and stakeholder expectations should require fail-over or
    rollback plans as part of the cutover planning process. Without the ability to recognize failure and quickly recover,
    business operations may be put at significant risk.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Develop the fail-over or rollback plan with a realistic timeline that leaves time to accommodate delays due to even
    minor unforeseen circumstances. These “unknowns” can result in significant project overruns, as the dependencies
    between various implementation activities tend to compound schedule slippage and resource costs because team members
    are not effectively utilized. Develop and adhere to a realistic cutover schedule that fully considers the complexity of
    the system customization and also helps management prioritize implementation of system features the business needs most
    – and most immediately.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="c4e1c180"></attribute>
<attribute name="usageGuidance" nodeId="4fa5aed4"></attribute>
<attribute name="refinedDescription" nodeId="4b0e4d5a">&lt;p&gt;
    The&amp;nbsp;rollback process refers to the activities needed to revert a deployed capability to the last-known-good
    configuration or to a manual backup process.&amp;nbsp; The&amp;nbsp;roll-back plan is the documentation of that process.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    A&amp;nbsp;rollback plan can be invoked for a variety of reasons, including corruption of the production code base;
    inoperable components; an unplanned undesirable effect of the release on other production systems; or for other
    reasons.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The rollback process should include activities and tasks to be followed in various trigger scenarios. Typical scenarios
    include one in which a “no-go” decision is made immediately prior to conversion to a new system or release, and when a
    “no-go” decision is made after a conversion has started.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    The rollback&amp;nbsp;process should include the steps to be taken to revert to the previous release, including operations
    activities, interim procedures and customer, stakeholder and employee communications.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    When starting to build the roll-back process, first determine if the enterprise has an&amp;nbsp;existing plan, and if so,
    whether it&amp;nbsp;needs to be updated, or decide that a new plan must be developed. Activities of the fail-over plan
    should be specific to the requirements of the current deployment.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    Determine if a rollback&amp;nbsp;plan exists and level of re-use:&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Substantiate whether the organization has a roll-back plan or process. An existing process may have been built as
        part of a contingency plan or for a previous release.&amp;nbsp;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        If there is a plan, review it to determine&amp;nbsp;reusability.&amp;nbsp;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Check with other related areas within the organization for plans that may be used as a template.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Update, add, or delete information as necessary.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        A benefit of using an existing plan is that it gives insight into how the organization thinks, performs or operates
        in crisis.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    If a rollback plan does not exist, or an existing plan can&apos;t be re-used, develop a new&amp;nbsp;process by documenting the
    risks that will be presented by the various trigger scenarios and identify actions to mitigate those risks.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Mitigation actions can include, for the first scenario, in which a “no-go” decision is made prior to conversion:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Notify key stakeholders to provide a justification to invoke the fail-over and seek approval for taking action
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Notify vendors and internal support personnel to stop the scheduled installation
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Notify business and conversion support personnel to cancel migration to new policies and procedures and schedule
        changes
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Notify customers of service disruption and schedule changes
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Notify employee and customer communication representatives
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Steps to be taken in the second scenario, in which a “no-go” decision is made after a conversion has started, include:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        De-install new application
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Revert to the previous state – the system scheduled for decommissioning
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Reinstate original procedures and forms
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Notify personnel to move to off-line procedures in certain situations (e.g., the re-install is not completed by a
        certain time)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Notify personnel of schedule changes
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Distribute customer communications
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Notify personnel to shift focus to contingency procedures
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
</Element>
